1
00:00:00,073 --> 00:00:01,113
If you're a software developer,

2
00:00:01,113 --> 00:00:02,753
I want you to think of the craziest edge

3
00:00:02,753 --> 00:00:03,633
cases in the world.

4
00:00:03,633 --> 00:00:05,133
The one thing that you thought,

5
00:00:05,133 --> 00:00:05,493
oh, yeah,

6
00:00:05,493 --> 00:00:07,753
there's no way a user would navigate and

7
00:00:07,753 --> 00:00:09,613
navigate and navigate and end up in this kind

8
00:00:09,613 --> 00:00:10,633
of situation.

9
00:00:10,633 --> 00:00:11,393
I promise you,

10
00:00:11,393 --> 00:00:13,433
with everything that I have and everything

11
00:00:13,433 --> 00:00:14,353
that I owe,

12
00:00:14,353 --> 00:00:15,793
someone is going to report an issue.

13
00:00:15,793 --> 00:00:17,277
And it's going to be

14
00:00:17,277 --> 00:00:19,347
exactly that edge case that you never thought

15
00:00:19,347 --> 00:00:20,253
of.

16
00:00:20,253 --> 00:00:21,693
It doesn't even have to be something big.

17
00:00:21,693 --> 00:00:23,633
You don't even have to have a lot of

18
00:00:23,633 --> 00:00:24,413
users

19
00:00:24,413 --> 00:00:26,683
for edge cases that you didn't

20
00:00:26,683 --> 00:00:28,183
write for to exist.

21
00:00:28,183 --> 00:00:29,133
Usually when we build,

22
00:00:29,133 --> 00:00:30,273
we create a

23
00:00:30,273 --> 00:00:31,333
direct simple path,

24
00:00:31,333 --> 00:00:32,393
you know?

25
00:00:32,393 --> 00:00:33,973
And just before that,

26
00:00:33,973 --> 00:00:34,353
you know,

27
00:00:34,353 --> 00:00:35,593
the user fills a form,

28
00:00:35,593 --> 00:00:36,633
they click a button,

29
00:00:36,633 --> 00:00:37,860
and then they wait.

30
00:00:37,860 --> 00:00:38,700
But it

31
00:00:38,700 --> 00:00:39,290
sounds straightforward,

32
00:00:39,290 --> 00:00:40,530
but it's not.

33
00:00:40,530 --> 00:00:41,820
Real users don't

34
00:00:41,820 --> 00:00:42,430
always do that.

35
00:00:42,430 --> 00:00:44,880
Real users don't just always fill the form and

36
00:00:44,880 --> 00:00:46,390
click the button and wait.

37
00:00:46,390 --> 00:00:47,960
Real users double-click,

38
00:00:47,960 --> 00:00:49,110
like ta-ta-ta-ta-ta-ta-ta.

39
00:00:49,363 --> 00:00:50,213
They click a lot.

40
00:00:50,213 --> 00:00:52,173
Real users open different tabs.

41
00:00:52,173 --> 00:00:54,513
Real users network drop halfway.

42
00:00:54,513 --> 00:00:55,653
Real users

43
00:00:55,653 --> 00:00:57,813
leave the page open for two hours,

44
00:00:57,813 --> 00:00:59,613
and they go and then they come back.

45
00:00:59,613 --> 00:01:00,953
And they're not doing anything strange.

46
00:01:00,953 --> 00:01:02,763
They are just being users.

47
00:01:02,763 --> 00:01:04,313
One of the most common edge cases in

48
00:01:04,313 --> 00:01:04,933
production

49
00:01:04,933 --> 00:01:06,633
is the duplicate submission.

50
00:01:06,633 --> 00:01:07,353
If, for example,

51
00:01:07,353 --> 00:01:08,453
in an online shop,

52
00:01:08,453 --> 00:01:10,413
someone clicks place order,

53
00:01:10,413 --> 00:01:11,853
and maybe the page

54
00:01:11,853 --> 00:01:12,933
feels a little slow,

55
00:01:12,933 --> 00:01:14,233
they click it again.

56
00:01:14,233 --> 00:01:16,253
Now our server gets

57
00:01:16,253 --> 00:01:16,853
two requests,

58
00:01:16,853 --> 00:01:18,753
and it creates two orders.

59
00:01:18,753 --> 00:01:20,313
And if we charge on each order,

60
00:01:20,313 --> 00:01:21,633
you can charge the user twice.

61
00:01:21,633 --> 00:01:23,313
It doesn't even need a double-click.

62
00:01:23,313 --> 00:01:24,893
The phone can lose the network after the

63
00:01:24,893 --> 00:01:27,193
request was sent and the app can retry.

64
00:01:27,193 --> 00:01:28,393
Or they can refresh the page,

65
00:01:28,393 --> 00:01:29,633
and then the browser asks them if they want

66
00:01:29,633 --> 00:01:31,813
to send the form again and they say yes.

67
00:01:31,813 --> 00:01:33,993
Disabling the button after the first click

68
00:01:33,993 --> 00:01:34,833
helps with this,

69
00:01:34,833 --> 00:01:36,403
but it only covers the double-click part.

70
00:01:36,403 --> 00:01:38,013
It does nothing for like maybe a retry

71
00:01:38,013 --> 00:01:39,213
or a second tab.

72
00:01:39,213 --> 00:01:41,493
The fix that is common for this is using

73
00:01:41,493 --> 00:01:42,503
an idempotency

74
00:01:42,503 --> 00:01:43,353
key.

75
00:01:43,353 --> 00:01:44,643
Basically, an idempotency

76
00:01:44,643 --> 00:01:46,713
key puts things in place such

77
00:01:46,713 --> 00:01:49,613
that doing something twice has the same

78
00:01:49,613 --> 00:01:52,053
result, as doing it just once.

79
00:01:52,183 --> 00:01:52,833
Stripe's

80
00:01:52,833 --> 00:01:55,833
API uses the same idea with an idempotency

81
00:01:55,833 --> 00:01:56,413
key header.

82
00:01:56,413 --> 00:01:57,793
So a retried

83
00:01:57,793 --> 00:01:59,473
request doesn't create a second payment.

84
00:01:59,473 --> 00:02:01,353
Another common edge case in production is a

85
00:02:01,353 --> 00:02:02,033
race condition.

86
00:02:02,033 --> 00:02:03,713
A race condition is when two things happen at

87
00:02:03,713 --> 00:02:04,573
the same time.

88
00:02:04,573 --> 00:02:06,483
And your code just assume that they would

89
00:02:06,483 --> 00:02:08,257
happen one after the other.

90
00:02:08,257 --> 00:02:09,287
was this researcher

91
00:02:09,287 --> 00:02:11,587
called Egor that found one of these race

92
00:02:11,587 --> 00:02:13,167
conditions in Starbucks

93
00:02:13,167 --> 00:02:14,687
gift cards.

94
00:02:15,027 --> 00:02:17,367
So he had gift cards worth $15

95
00:02:17,367 --> 00:02:18,447
in total,

96
00:02:18,447 --> 00:02:20,827
and he sent the same

97
00:02:20,827 --> 00:02:21,917
balance transfer twice,

98
00:02:21,917 --> 00:02:23,127
at the same time,

99
00:02:23,127 --> 00:02:24,587
from two different sessions.

100
00:02:24,587 --> 00:02:25,947
Both transfers went through,

101
00:02:25,947 --> 00:02:28,227
and his card ended up with $20

102
00:02:28,267 --> 00:02:29,187
between them.

103
00:02:29,187 --> 00:02:31,127
So each request checked the balance,

104
00:02:31,127 --> 00:02:32,127
saw that it was enough money,

105
00:02:32,127 --> 00:02:32,967
and moved it.

106
00:02:32,967 --> 00:02:34,587
Neither request was there for the other one to

107
00:02:34,587 --> 00:02:35,327
finish.

108
00:02:35,327 --> 00:02:37,017
The same thing can happen in your app.

109
00:02:37,017 --> 00:02:37,587
For example,

110
00:02:37,587 --> 00:02:39,037
in our online shop,

111
00:02:39,037 --> 00:02:41,787
say we have one item left in stock,

112
00:02:41,787 --> 00:02:45,027
two people click buy in the exact same second,

113
00:02:45,087 --> 00:02:47,197
both requests check the stock,

114
00:02:47,197 --> 00:02:48,807
both see one item left,

115
00:02:48,807 --> 00:02:50,297
both create an order,

116
00:02:50,297 --> 00:02:51,887
and now we have sold an item that we

117
00:02:51,887 --> 00:02:52,967
do not have.

118
00:02:52,967 --> 00:02:54,547
What do we do about this?

119
00:02:54,547 --> 00:02:56,567
So if the two requests arrive at the same

120
00:02:56,567 --> 00:02:57,967
time, only one of them can take the last

121
00:02:57,967 --> 00:03:00,687
item. The other one gets zero rows updated,

122
00:03:00,687 --> 00:03:02,497
and that's how you know it's sold out.

123
00:03:02,497 --> 00:03:03,887
A lot of edge

124
00:03:03,887 --> 00:03:04,827
case

125
00:03:04,907 --> 00:03:06,747
situations in production can also happen with

126
00:03:06,747 --> 00:03:07,467
names

127
00:03:07,467 --> 00:03:08,167
because

128
00:03:08,167 --> 00:03:10,837
we sometimes build names featuring like

129
00:03:10,837 --> 00:03:11,947
default names.

130
00:03:11,947 --> 00:03:13,207
Take the name O'Brien,

131
00:03:13,207 --> 00:03:13,887
for example,

132
00:03:13,887 --> 00:03:15,687
the apostrophe is the same character that

133
00:03:15,687 --> 00:03:17,687
starts an empty piece of text in SQL.

134
00:03:17,687 --> 00:03:19,647
So if your code glues the name straight into

135
00:03:19,647 --> 00:03:20,647
the query,

136
00:03:20,647 --> 00:03:21,607
the apostrophe

137
00:03:21,607 --> 00:03:22,847
ends the text early,

138
00:03:22,847 --> 00:03:23,937
and the query breaks.

139
00:03:23,937 --> 00:03:26,337
And if an apostrophe can break your query by

140
00:03:26,337 --> 00:03:28,387
accident, it means that someone can type

141
00:03:28,387 --> 00:03:29,867
something after the apostrophe

142
00:03:29,867 --> 00:03:30,987
on purpose,

143
00:03:30,987 --> 00:03:32,627
and that will be SQL injection.

144
00:03:32,627 --> 00:03:33,347
In 2016,

145
00:03:33,347 --> 00:03:35,447
the BBC wrote about a woman called Jennifer

146
00:03:35,447 --> 00:03:36,207
Null.

147
00:03:36,207 --> 00:03:38,207
When Jennifer Null tries to buy a plane

148
00:03:38,207 --> 00:03:39,417
ticket, most websites

149
00:03:39,417 --> 00:03:41,677
tell her that she has left the surname field

150
00:03:41,677 --> 00:03:42,387
blank.

151
00:03:42,387 --> 00:03:43,707
Her surname is Null.

152
00:03:43,707 --> 00:03:45,817
The website sees the name Null and treats it

153
00:03:45,817 --> 00:03:47,067
as empty.

154
00:03:47,067 --> 00:03:48,117
And yeah,

155
00:03:48,117 --> 00:03:48,667
so Null,

156
00:03:48,667 --> 00:03:50,537
apart from even being in a name situation,

157
00:03:50,537 --> 00:03:52,747
is also an edge case that catches

158
00:03:52,747 --> 00:03:55,547
almost everyone because Null means no value.

159
00:03:55,547 --> 00:03:56,997
There was another story,

160
00:03:56,997 --> 00:03:58,667
I was told at a conference,

161
00:03:58,667 --> 00:03:59,977
where a security

162
00:03:59,977 --> 00:04:01,877
researcher got a personal

163
00:04:01,877 --> 00:04:04,147
license plate in California that said NULL.

164
00:04:04,147 --> 00:04:05,447
Like that was the license plate,

165
00:04:05,447 --> 00:04:07,667
NULL. Then he started getting parking tickets

166
00:04:07,667 --> 00:04:08,947
over $12,000

167
00:04:08,947 --> 00:04:11,213
in fines that were not his tickets.

168
00:04:11,213 --> 00:04:12,923
There were tickets where no plate number had

169
00:04:12,923 --> 00:04:13,273
been recorded.

170
00:04:13,273 --> 00:04:15,713
And in the system that processes the tickets,

171
00:04:15,713 --> 00:04:18,163
those were now linked to a plate called NULL.

172
00:04:18,163 --> 00:04:20,383
So all of them landed on his plate.

173
00:04:20,383 --> 00:04:22,193
So this is another null situation.

174
00:04:22,193 --> 00:04:23,533
Another one that is common,

175
00:04:23,533 --> 00:04:24,983
another thing that can cause very interesting

176
00:04:24,983 --> 00:04:25,913
edge cases

177
00:04:25,913 --> 00:04:26,773
situation in production

178
00:04:26,773 --> 00:04:27,860
is leap years.

179
00:04:27,973 --> 00:04:29,763
Leap years are an edge case that will show

180
00:04:29,763 --> 00:04:30,673
up every four years,

181
00:04:30,673 --> 00:04:32,793
which is exactly why they get missed.

182
00:04:33,240 --> 00:04:34,620
On the 29th of February,

183
00:04:34,620 --> 00:04:35,472
Microsoft's cloud,

184
00:04:35,472 --> 00:04:36,138
Windows Azure,

185
00:04:36,138 --> 00:04:39,740
had an outage because of a leap year bug.

186
00:04:39,740 --> 00:04:41,620
Part of their system made

187
00:04:41,620 --> 00:04:42,510
security

188
00:04:42,510 --> 00:04:44,350
certificates that were valid for one year.

189
00:04:44,350 --> 00:04:46,970
And the system worked out the end dates by

190
00:04:46,970 --> 00:04:48,820
taking today's date and just adding one to the

191
00:04:48,820 --> 00:04:49,680
year.

192
00:04:49,680 --> 00:04:51,500
So on the 29th of February,

193
00:04:51,500 --> 00:04:52,970
2012, that system,

194
00:04:52,970 --> 00:04:55,380
that logic gave 29th of February,

195
00:04:55,380 --> 00:04:57,380
2013, and that date doesn't exist.

196
00:04:57,380 --> 00:04:59,320
So creating the certificate

197
00:04:59,320 --> 00:05:01,860
failed and part of Azure went down with it.

198
00:05:01,860 --> 00:05:02,480
So yeah,

199
00:05:02,480 --> 00:05:04,500
this is something to think about as well,

200
00:05:04,500 --> 00:05:05,440
leap year bugs.

201
00:05:05,440 --> 00:05:07,920
Another edge case that can cause issues:

202
00:05:07,920 --> 00:05:09,600
time zones and daylight saving.

203
00:05:09,600 --> 00:05:11,020
There's also an edge case

204
00:05:11,020 --> 00:05:12,510
in production that a lot of people do not

205
00:05:12,510 --> 00:05:13,180
think about.

206
00:05:13,180 --> 00:05:14,200
In Estonia where I live,

207
00:05:14,200 --> 00:05:16,100
the clock changes twice in a year.

208
00:05:16,100 --> 00:05:17,710
On the 29th of March,

209
00:05:17,710 --> 00:05:20,130
the clocks in Tallinn jump from 3 a.m.

210
00:05:20,130 --> 00:05:21,000
straight to 4 a.m.

211
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:22,380
So 3:30

212
00:05:22,380 --> 00:05:23,790
that morning never happened.

213
00:05:23,790 --> 00:05:25,540
The same way on the 25th

214
00:05:25,540 --> 00:05:26,180
of

215
00:05:26,180 --> 00:05:27,240
October,

216
00:05:27,540 --> 00:05:28,380
the clock

217
00:05:28,380 --> 00:05:29,420
went from 4 a.m.

218
00:05:29,420 --> 00:05:30,360
back to 3 a.m.

219
00:05:30,360 --> 00:05:31,650
So 3:30

220
00:05:31,737 --> 00:05:32,767
happened twice.

221
00:05:32,767 --> 00:05:35,407
Now imagine our online shop sends a daily

222
00:05:35,407 --> 00:05:36,704
sales report at 3:30,

223
00:05:36,704 --> 00:05:38,227
Tallinn time.

224
00:05:38,227 --> 00:05:39,827
On one day of the year,

225
00:05:39,827 --> 00:05:40,437
that

226
00:05:40,437 --> 00:05:41,717
job would not run.

227
00:05:41,717 --> 00:05:42,567
On another day of the year,

228
00:05:42,567 --> 00:05:43,697
it would run twice.

229
00:05:43,697 --> 00:05:45,027
So these are things to consider.

230
00:05:45,027 --> 00:05:47,327
Another common edge case is the Unicode bug.

231
00:05:47,327 --> 00:05:49,047
Unicode is the edge case that breaks names

232
00:05:49,047 --> 00:05:50,807
with accents and tone marks.

233
00:05:50,807 --> 00:05:52,757
A lot of Yoruba names have this.

234
00:05:53,147 --> 00:05:54,374
Take Adébáyọ̀.

235
00:05:54,374 --> 00:05:55,487
Adébáyọ̀, for example,

236
00:05:55,487 --> 00:05:57,627
you can see seven letters,

237
00:05:57,627 --> 00:05:58,747
but some

238
00:05:58,747 --> 00:05:59,767
functions count bytes,

239
00:05:59,767 --> 00:06:01,127
not letters,

240
00:06:01,127 --> 00:06:02,547
right? So in this case,

241
00:06:02,547 --> 00:06:03,687
you would have 13,

242
00:06:03,687 --> 00:06:06,060
because letters like é and ọ̀

243
00:06:06,060 --> 00:06:07,880
take more than one byte each.

244
00:06:07,880 --> 00:06:09,920
So if we check the name must be at

245
00:06:09,920 --> 00:06:11,630
most 20 characters.

246
00:06:11,630 --> 00:06:13,200
If we check that with the wrong function,

247
00:06:13,200 --> 00:06:15,000
you can reject actual names.

248
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:16,670
So the fix for this is usually to count

249
00:06:16,670 --> 00:06:18,820
with a function that counts characters and to

250
00:06:18,820 --> 00:06:20,900
normalize the text before it saves.

251
00:06:20,900 --> 00:06:23,730
Hyrum's law is about a different kind of edge

252
00:06:23,730 --> 00:06:26,460
case. It's the behavior in your app that you

253
00:06:26,460 --> 00:06:27,720
actually never promised.

254
00:06:27,720 --> 00:06:29,780
It's named after Hyrum Wright.

255
00:06:29,780 --> 00:06:31,280
Hyrum's law states

256
00:06:31,280 --> 00:06:32,110
that with a

257
00:06:32,110 --> 00:06:34,240
sufficient number of users of an API,

258
00:06:34,240 --> 00:06:36,080
it does not matter what you promise in the

259
00:06:36,080 --> 00:06:37,140
contract. All

260
00:06:37,140 --> 00:06:39,500
observable behaviors of your system will be

261
00:06:39,500 --> 00:06:41,187
depended on by someone.

262
00:06:41,187 --> 00:06:43,507
So let's say our online shop API happens to

263
00:06:43,507 --> 00:06:45,537
return products in the order that we had it.

264
00:06:45,537 --> 00:06:46,607
We didn't choose that.

265
00:06:46,607 --> 00:06:49,027
The query just happened to come back that way.

266
00:06:49,027 --> 00:06:50,757
Now, if someone is using our API to show

267
00:06:50,757 --> 00:06:51,787
products in their app,

268
00:06:51,787 --> 00:06:53,757
and they're showing the newest products by

269
00:06:53,757 --> 00:06:54,777
taking the last

270
00:06:54,777 --> 00:06:55,947
product in the list,

271
00:06:55,947 --> 00:06:56,727
then if we,

272
00:06:56,727 --> 00:06:57,647
on our end,

273
00:06:57,647 --> 00:07:00,107
maybe add an index or change the query,

274
00:07:00,107 --> 00:07:02,417
the order will change and the app will break.

275
00:07:02,417 --> 00:07:03,087
To them,

276
00:07:03,087 --> 00:07:04,227
we've broken something.

277
00:07:04,227 --> 00:07:04,587
To us,

278
00:07:04,587 --> 00:07:06,197
we didn't break anything because we never

279
00:07:06,197 --> 00:07:07,187
promised that behavior.

280
00:07:07,187 --> 00:07:08,727
But because they are used

281
00:07:08,727 --> 00:07:09,627
to getting that behavior,

282
00:07:09,627 --> 00:07:11,417
they now think that we've broken

283
00:07:11,417 --> 00:07:12,843
something. So this is also

284
00:07:12,843 --> 00:07:14,403
the kind of edge case that you also have

285
00:07:14,403 --> 00:07:15,373
to consider.

286
00:07:15,373 --> 00:07:15,873
To be honest,

287
00:07:15,873 --> 00:07:16,803
there's so much more.

288
00:07:16,803 --> 00:07:18,513
You can't undo every single one,

